{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>And you shall make its dishes and its jars.</b> Its dishes — this is a mold, made in the shape of the bread. And the bread was made in the shape of an open box on its two sides, with a base below, and bending upward on either side like walls — and therefore it is called לֶחֶם הַפָּנִים, because it has 'faces' looking this way and that toward the sides of the House, here and here. And one sets its length along the width of the table, and its walls stand upright against the edge of the table. And it had a golden mold and an iron mold: in the iron one it is baked, and when he takes it out of the oven he puts it in the golden one until the next day, the Sabbath, when they arrange it on the table. And that mold is called קְעָרָה." "<b>And its jars.</b> Bowls in which they place two portions of frankincense for the two handfuls of frankincense that are placed upon the two arrangements, as it says ״וְנָתַתָּ עַל הַמַּעֲרֶכֶת לְבֹנָה זַכָּה״ (Leviticus 24)." "<b>And its supports.</b> They are like hollow half-reeds split lengthwise (Menachot 96). Their form is made of gold, and one arranges three upon each loaf, so that the other loaf rests upon those reeds, and they separate loaf from loaf so that air may enter between them and they not grow moldy. And in Arabic every hollow thing is called קסוא." "<b>And its holders.</b> Its Targum is וּמְכִילָתֵיהּ. They are supports like golden pegs, standing on the ground and rising high above the table, matching the height of the bread arrangement, and split into five branches one above the other, and the ends of the reeds between loaf and loaf rest upon those branches, so that the weight of the upper loaves not press upon the lower ones and break them. And the term מְכִילָתֵיהּ means 'its bearers,' as in ״נִלְאֵיתִי הָכִיל״ (Jeremiah 6). But the term מְנַקִּיּוֹת — I do not know how it applies to supports. And some of the Sages of Israel say: its קְשׂוֹתָיו — these are the supports, which strengthen it and hold it so it not break; and its מְנַקִּיּוֹת — these are the reeds, which clear the way so it not grow moldy (Menachot 97). But Onkelos, who translated וּמְכִילָתֵיהּ, would differ with the one who says מְנַקִּיּוֹת are the supports." "<b>With which it is covered.</b> That are covered with them. And regarding קְשׂוֹתָיו it says אֲשֶׁר יֻסַּךְ — because upon them there was a kind of covering and overlay. And elsewhere it says ״וְאֵת קְשׂוֹת הַנָּסֶךְ״ (Numbers 4). And both this and that — יֻסַּךְ and הַנָּסֶךְ — are terms of covering and overlay."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:54:40.095580118Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}